# SET-PIECE: the lookout. THE FIRST PATCH -- a piece narrower than the bridge.
#
# Every other piece in the library spans the whole canvas and owns its rows
# outright. This one is five columns wide, so the generator drops it somewhere
# across the deck and ordinary terrain runs past on both sides -- the footprint
# `design_ideas/world_generation.md` has been asking for since M17 and nothing
# could express until now.
#
# THE COMPOSITION IS THE CLIMB AND WHAT IT COSTS. Two units of high ground with
# one way up, and the way up is a two-row ramp on ONE side: taking the lookout
# means committing to a direction and spending four rows of walking, and coming
# off it means dropping. From up there you can see and be seen, which is the
# whole reason to want it and the whole reason not to.
#
# IT CARRIES ITS OWN RAMP, and that is not decoration. A raised patch with no way
# up is marooned deck and `_check_orphans` refuses the section outright -- so a
# generator that assembled a platform and a ramp separately would produce runs
# that silently lose their pieces. M23 phase 1 learnt this by authoring a tower
# with one ramp row for a two-unit climb and watching six cells come out
# unreachable. The ramp being INSIDE the piece is what makes the piece a
# relationship rather than three primitives that happen to be adjacent.
#
# ENTRY AND EXIT ROWS AT HEIGHT 0, across the full footprint. The terrain either
# side of a patch is at the plateau the piece was stamped at, so a patch whose
# edge rows sat higher would be a step nobody authored, running down both of its
# long sides.
#
# piece_exit = 0 BY RULE, not by choice. Terrain flows past a patch on both
# sides at the plateau height; a patch that claimed to raise the deck would
# desync the running height from the ground either side of itself.

name = piece_lookout
base_height = 0
width = 5
length = 6
piece_exit = 0
tags = foot, piece, environmental

[deck]
.....
./...
./...
.....
.....
.....

[height]
00000
01000
02000
02220
02220
00000

[content]
.....
.....
.....
.....
.....
.....
